<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roman Republic</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Roman Republic was the system of government of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> from the expulsion of the kings, traditionally dated to 509 BCE, until the rise of Augustus in the late first century BCE. Governed by elected magistrates and the Senate, it expanded across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, defeating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and the Hellenistic kingdoms. It laid the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> and, as accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a> note, overcame the Macedonian kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roman-republic.jpg" alt="Roman Republic" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ifly6 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_republic,_territory_44_BC.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> had been ruled by monarchs elected for life by the Senate. Traditional histories say the last, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarquinius-superbus">Tarquin the Proud</a>, was expelled in 509 BCE after his son Sextus Tarquinius raped a noblewoman, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucretia">Lucretia</a>, and that a revolution led by the semi-mythical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucius-junius-brutus">Lucius Junius Brutus</a> transferred the king's powers to two consuls elected for one year, each able to veto his colleague. Most modern scholarship describes these accounts as the quasi-mythological detailing of an aristocratic coup within Tarquin's own family, or a consequence of an Etruscan occupation of Rome, rather than a popular revolution.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Republican <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> was in a state of near-perpetual war. After the Gallic sack of Rome it conquered most of the Italian peninsula in a century. It fought three wars against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and defeated it at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-zama">Battle of Zama</a> in 202 BCE, then defeated Philip V and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perseus-of-macedon">Perseus of Macedon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antiochus-iii-the-great">Antiochus III</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucid-empire">Seleucid Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arverni">Arverni</a>. At home the plebs achieved political equality with the patricians by the 4th century BCE, ending the Conflict of the Orders peacefully.</p><p>From 133 BCE the late Republic saw substantial domestic strife. The Social War between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> and its Italian allies over citizenship expanded civil violence, and mass <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> contributed to three Servile Wars. Powerful semi-autonomous generals led to civil wars, first between Marius and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sulla">Sulla</a> and in 49 BCE between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pompey">Pompey</a>. Erich S. Gruen holds that civil war caused the fall of the republic, not the reverse. It ended in 27 BCE with the establishment of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The constitution was an unwritten, evolving set of principles passed down through precedent. There were annual elections, but the system was an elective <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oligarchy">oligarchy</a>, with powerful families largely monopolising the senior magistracies. The Senate's decrees were officially advice, yet magistrates usually followed them, and it came to control finances and external affairs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consul">Consuls</a> and praetors held imperium, the authority to command an army. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">Census</a> figures report 130,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in 509 BCE and 4,937,000 in 14 CE. From 107 BCE all citizens, whatever their wealth, could enter the army, which became paid and attracted the poorest.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Roman Republic” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When the <b>Roman Republic</b> destroyed Carthage at the end of the Third Punic War in 146 BCE, it turned the city's remaining territory in what is now northern Tunisia into the province of Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The territory of Etruscan civilisation reached its greatest extent around 500 BCE, shortly after the Roman Kingdom became the <b>Roman Republic</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/etruscan-league">Etruscan League · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Pontus mattered most as the base of Mithridates VI, whose wars against Rome lasted, with intervals, from 89 to 63 BCE and shaped the politics of the late <b>Roman Republic</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-pontus">Kingdom of Pontus · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Numidia, united by Masinissa after the Second Punic War, was the first great Berber kingdom, spreading Punic urban culture inland, helping Rome destroy Carthage and then, under Jugurtha, fighting a war that exposed the corruption of the <b>Roman Republic</b> before its absorption into Roman Africa in 46 BCE.</blockquote><a href="/empire/numidia">Numidia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…annexed Avignon and the Comtat Venaissin in 1791, Napoleon forced Pius VI to cede the Legations of Bologna, Ferrara and Romagna at the Treaty of Tolentino in 1797, and in 1798 French troops proclaimed a <b>Roman Republic</b> and carried the pope into exile, where he died in 1799.</blockquote><a href="/empire/papal-states">Papal States · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Roman Empire replaced the <b>Roman Republic</b>, itself the product of centuries of expansion from a small city-state on the banks of the Tiber in central Italy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-empire">Roman Empire · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
